1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,400
 Polyethylene is the first thermoplastic on our list, and it's for good reason.

2
00:00:04,400 --> 00:00:09,600
 Polyethylene is the most commonly used, most commonly produced thermoplastic in the world,

3
00:00:09,600 --> 00:00:13,360
 accounting for nearly 30% of global plastic use and production.

4
00:00:13,360 --> 00:00:17,520
 If you like, polyethylene is to plastics what mild steel is to metals.

5
00:00:17,520 --> 00:00:21,520
 It's the characteristic plastic. When you think about the properties of a plastic,

6
00:00:21,520 --> 00:00:26,320
 you're probably actually thinking about the properties of polyethylene because it's everywhere.

7
00:00:26,320 --> 00:00:31,280
 The reasons why polyethylene are so widespread as similar to the reasons why mild steel

8
00:00:31,280 --> 00:00:36,080
 is so widespread. Largely through inertia, but also through the tunability of its properties,

9
00:00:36,080 --> 00:00:42,080
 polyethylene is extremely cheap to produce. As the name suggests, polyethylene is polymerized

10
00:00:42,080 --> 00:00:47,520
 from the ethylene monomer, one that's derived from fossil fuels. A polyethylene chain consists

11
00:00:47,520 --> 00:00:52,720
 of a carbon backbone, its organic, and it has hydrogen fingers. And a range of variants

12
00:00:52,720 --> 00:00:57,520
 of polyethylene have been developed for a bunch of different design circumstances. So-called

13
00:00:57,520 --> 00:01:05,200
 low-density polyethylene, LDPE is formed by inducing polyethylene chains to branch without cross-linking.

14
00:01:05,200 --> 00:01:09,040
 This branching makes the chains spread out and essentially reduces their density.

15
00:01:09,040 --> 00:01:12,240
 And in doing so, weakens the effective strength of the Van der Waals bonds,

16
00:01:12,240 --> 00:01:17,520
 reduced to the contact area over which they can act. The result is that LDPE is quite a flexible

17
00:01:17,520 --> 00:01:22,320
 product, and for that reason it's used in shopping bags and snap-on lids. A variation on this

18
00:01:22,320 --> 00:01:28,720
 particular flavor called linear low-density polyethylene, LLDPE, also is very flexible,

19
00:01:28,720 --> 00:01:34,080
 is extremely flexible, owing to the extremely short branches that it makes. You'll often see

20
00:01:34,080 --> 00:01:39,600
 LLDPE used in places that require exceptional ductility, exceptional flexibility,

21
00:01:39,600 --> 00:01:45,200
 things like cling wrap, for example. Finally, high-density polyethylene, HDPE is

22
00:01:45,200 --> 00:01:49,920
 kind of as the name suggests where the chains don't form any branches at all, and as a result

23
00:01:49,920 --> 00:01:54,880
 can pack reasonably densely. This dense packing of polymer chains means that the Van der Waals

24
00:01:54,880 --> 00:02:00,880
 bonds of HDPE have essentially more surface area to act over, and this makes it both stronger and

25
00:02:00,880 --> 00:02:05,760
 stiffer than its low-density counterparts. For this reason, you'll normally see HDPE used in places

26
00:02:05,760 --> 00:02:10,400
 that require a little bit more strength or stiffness, things like pipe work and fuel tanks.

27
00:02:10,400 --> 00:02:15,280
 Now, I've used the word density throughout here in inverted commas, because while the density of

28
00:02:15,280 --> 00:02:20,560
 the chains in the different flavours of polyethylene changes, the packing density changes,

29
00:02:20,560 --> 00:02:25,680
 the effective density of the bulk polymers actually remains the same. The reasons behind this are a

30
00:02:25,680 --> 00:02:29,840
 little complicated, but essentially it turns out that irrespective of the way you pack it,

31
00:02:29,840 --> 00:02:33,920
 polyethylene, as a bulk, is going to have roughly the same density no matter what.

32
00:02:33,920 --> 00:02:38,560
 And this is just one of the many examples where somebody somewhere has co-opted a word that had

33
00:02:38,560 --> 00:02:44,080
 a very specific meaning in engineering and introduced some ambiguity that now we all have to live with.

34
00:02:44,080 --> 00:02:48,160
 Importantly, these different flavours of polyethylene don't necessarily arise because of

35
00:02:48,160 --> 00:02:52,560
 differences in the chemistry of the polymer. Instead, they arise as a result of different

36
00:02:52,560 --> 00:02:57,120
 manufacturing processes that it's put through. Low-density polyethylene and linear low-density

37
00:02:57,120 --> 00:03:01,600
 polyethylene are normally made at low temperatures and high pressures through processes like

38
00:03:01,600 --> 00:03:06,640
 extrusion. And high-density polyethylene is normally made low pressures and high temperatures,

39
00:03:06,640 --> 00:03:11,120
 where the high temperature provides sufficient energy and motivation and vibration for all of

40
00:03:11,200 --> 00:03:16,560
 the chains to pack more densely. Being the most common plastic, polyethylene is also the most

41
00:03:16,560 --> 00:03:22,400
 commonly recycled plastic. In fact, most manufacturing facilities are set up to only recycled

42
00:03:22,400 --> 00:03:28,960
 polyethylene. For this reason, HDPE, LDP, and another variant, P-E-T, polyethylene, to wrap

43
00:03:28,960 --> 00:03:33,840
 for it are normally carefully separated from other plastics for the purpose of being recycled.

44
00:03:33,840 --> 00:03:37,760
 After separation, these plastics are normally separated from each other before being shredded

45
00:03:37,760 --> 00:03:42,400
 and melted down into noodles for recycling into other products. We'll talk more about

46
00:03:42,400 --> 00:03:47,760
 the implications of this when we meet some other plastics in the following videos.
